/* Generated by tools/build.js from data/project-plus.js. Edit that file, not this one. */
CertHub.addQuestions("project-plus", [["pp1",0,1,"Halfway through a CRM rollout, a sales director emails the project manager asking for an extra reporting dashboard. What should the project manager do first?",["Log a change request and assess its impact","Add the dashboard to the current sprint so the director stays engaged","Decline, because scope cannot change once the plan is approved","Ask the developers to build it quietly in their spare time"],0,"Every scope change goes through change control: document it, analyze its effect on scope, schedule, cost and risk, then send it for a decision. Simply adding it (or doing it quietly) is scope creep, and flatly refusing ignores that approved changes are a normal part of projects.","Domain 1.0: Change control",[null,"Adding the dashboard straight into the current work skips impact analysis and approval, which is exactly how scope creep starts.","Approved plans can change; refusing outright ignores the change control process, which exists to evaluate requests like this.","Building unapproved work quietly hides its cost and risk from decision makers and is still uncontrolled scope creep."],1],["pp2",0,1,"A change request to move a go-live date by three weeks has been fully impact-assessed. In a typical change control process, who approves or rejects it?",["The change control board","The developer who will do the work","The vendor's account manager","The end users who will test it"],0,"The change control board (or whoever the change management plan names) has the authority to approve or reject changes. Team members and vendors provide input to the impact assessment but do not approve changes to the baselines.","Domain 1.0: Change control",[null,"The developer helps estimate the impact but has no authority to change the project's approved baselines.","A vendor's account manager may be affected by the change but does not approve changes to the buyer's project.","End users may request or test changes, but approval belongs to the change control board or named authority."],1],["pp3",0,1,"Users keep asking developers directly for small extra features, and the developers add them without any formal approval. Over months, the delivery date slips. What is this an example of?",["Fast-tracking","Resource smoothing","Rolling-wave planning","Scope creep"],3,"Scope creep is uncontrolled growth in scope through unapproved additions. Fast-tracking overlaps activities, smoothing adjusts resources within float, and rolling-wave planning details near-term work while leaving later work at a high level.","Domain 1.0: Types of change",["Fast-tracking is a deliberate schedule compression technique that overlaps planned activities, not the addition of unapproved features.","Resource smoothing shifts work within float to even out workloads; it has nothing to do with scope growth.","Rolling-wave planning details near-term work and leaves later work high level; it is a planning technique, not unapproved additions.",null],1],["pp4",0,1,"The change control board approves a request that adds two weeks and $15,000 to a project. What should the project manager do next?",["Close the request immediately and file it without any further action","Restart the business case analysis from the discovery phase","Update the affected baselines and documents and notify stakeholders","Wait until project closing to record the change in lessons learned"],2,"An approved change is only useful if the plan reflects it: update the scope, schedule and cost baselines, the change log and other documents, and communicate the decision. Filing it away or waiting until closing leaves the team working from an outdated plan.","Domain 1.0: Change control",["An approved change still has to be reflected in the plan; closing it without updates leaves the team working from outdated baselines.","An approved change does not normally require redoing the business case from scratch; the impact was already assessed.",null,"Waiting until closing means the schedule and budget are wrong for the rest of the project; lessons learned do not replace updating the plan."],2],["pp5",0,1,"A project team buys an insurance policy to cover damage to servers while they are shipped to a new data center. Which risk response is this?",["Avoid","Mitigate","Accept","Transfer"],3,"Transferring shifts the financial impact of a risk to a third party, and insurance is the classic example. Mitigation would reduce the chance of damage (better packing), and avoidance would remove the risk entirely (not shipping the servers).","Domain 1.0: Risk responses",["Avoiding the risk would mean removing it entirely, for example by not shipping the servers, not buying coverage for it.","Mitigation reduces probability or impact, such as better packing; insurance leaves the risk unchanged and moves who pays.","Acceptance means taking no action in advance, but buying insurance is an active response.",null],1],["pp6",0,1,"To reduce the chance that a weekend database migration fails, the project manager schedules two full rehearsal migrations in a test environment. Which risk response is being used?",["Transfer","Exploit","Mitigate","Avoid"],2,"Rehearsals lower the probability and impact of failure, which is mitigation. The risk still exists, so it is not avoided, and no third party takes it on, so it is not transferred. Exploit is a response to positive risks (opportunities).","Domain 1.0: Risk responses",["Transferring would shift the financial impact to a third party such as an insurer, while rehearsals keep the risk with the team.","Exploit is a response for positive risks (opportunities), not for reducing the chance of a failure.",null,"Avoiding would remove the risk entirely, for example by not migrating; rehearsals only reduce its likelihood and impact."],1],["pp7",0,1,"A risk has a low probability and a low impact, and any response would cost more than the damage it could cause. What is the most appropriate response?",["Accept it and keep it on the risk register","Transfer it to an insurer with a new policy","Escalate it to the steering committee for a decision","Avoid it by removing the related work from scope"],0,"Passive acceptance is appropriate when a response costs more than the risk is worth. The risk stays on the register so it can be watched in case its probability or impact changes. Escalation is for risks outside the project manager's authority.","Domain 1.0: Risk responses",[null,"Buying insurance costs money, which the question says is more than the risk is worth.","Escalation is for risks outside the project's scope or the project manager's authority, not low-priority risks the project can hold.","Cutting scope to avoid a minor, low-impact risk is out of proportion and would cost more than the risk itself."],2],["pp8",0,1,"A project manager asks the team to rate each identified risk as high, medium or low for probability and impact so the list can be prioritized quickly. Which activity is this?",["Quantitative risk analysis","Risk transfer","Monte Carlo simulation","Qualitative risk analysis"],3,"Qualitative analysis ranks risks with subjective scales such as high, medium and low, often plotted on a probability and impact matrix. Quantitative analysis puts numbers on the effect, for example with expected monetary value or Monte Carlo simulation.","Domain 1.0: Risk management",["Quantitative analysis puts numbers such as money or days on risks; rating them high, medium or low is qualitative.","Risk transfer is a response strategy that shifts impact to a third party, not a way to rank risks.","Monte Carlo simulation is a quantitative modeling technique, far more involved than simple high, medium and low ratings.",null],2],["pp9",0,1,"A newly formed project team has begun arguing about roles and how the work should be done, and productivity has dropped. According to Tuckman's model, which stage is the team in?",["Storming","Forming","Norming","Adjourning"],0,"Storming is the stage where conflict surfaces as people push back on roles and approaches. Forming is the polite, uncertain start; norming is when the team settles on shared ways of working; adjourning is when the team disbands.","Domain 1.0: Team dynamics",[null,"Forming is the polite, uncertain start of a team before disagreements surface.","Norming comes after conflict is resolved, when the team settles on shared ways of working.","Adjourning is the final stage, when the work ends and the team disbands."],1],["pp10",0,1,"During planning, a team member points out that the only vendor able to supply a specialized appliance may go out of business. Where should the project manager record this, along with an owner and a planned response?",["Issue log","Change log","Risk register","Lessons learned register"],2,"This is an uncertain future event, so it is a risk and belongs in the risk register with probability, impact, owner and response. The issue log is for problems already happening, and the change log tracks change requests.","Domain 1.0: Risk management",["The issue log tracks problems that are already happening; a vendor that might fail is still an uncertain future event.","The change log records change requests and decisions, not uncertain events and their planned responses.",null,"The lessons learned register captures insights for the future, not active risks with owners and responses."],1],["pp11",0,1,"Network switches that were due to arrive on Monday have not been delivered, and the installation team cannot start work. How should the project manager treat this?",["As an issue to log, assign an owner and resolve","As a risk to add to the register for later analysis","As a change request for the change control board","As an assumption to update in the project charter"],0,"The late delivery is already happening and blocking work, so it is an issue, not a risk. The project manager logs it, assigns an owner and a due date, and works it to resolution; a change request may follow if the fix changes a baseline.","Domain 1.0: Risk and issue management",[null,"The event has already happened and is blocking work, so it is no longer an uncertain risk.","A change request is only needed if the fix changes a baseline; the first step is logging and resolving the issue.","Assumptions are unproven beliefs recorded during planning; a missed delivery is a current problem to manage."],2],["pp12",0,1,"The project sponsor says she is overwhelmed by the detailed technical updates she receives every day and only wants a short weekly summary. Which document should the project manager update?",["Risk register","WBS dictionary","Communication plan","Quality management plan"],2,"The communication plan defines who gets what information, in what format, how often and by which method. Tailoring the sponsor's updates to a weekly executive summary is a communication plan change.","Domain 1.0: Communication management",["The risk register tracks uncertain events; it does not define how often or in what format the sponsor receives updates.","The WBS dictionary describes work packages in detail and has nothing to do with stakeholder reporting.",null,"The quality management plan covers quality standards and assurance, not the format and frequency of status updates."],2],["pp13",0,1,"A project team is spread across five time zones and needs to share daily progress without everyone being online at the same moment. Which approach fits best?",["Hold one daily video call at 9 a.m. headquarters time for all members","Schedule face-to-face meetings at each site every morning","Have the project manager phone each member every day","Post updates in a shared project channel or board"],3,"Asynchronous communication lets people read and respond when they are working, which suits teams across time zones. A fixed daily call forces some members to join at night, and one-to-one phone calls do not scale.","Domain 1.0: Communication methods",["One fixed call time forces members in distant time zones to join at night, so it does not suit a global team.","Face-to-face meetings at every site each morning are impractical and still would not bring the whole team together.","Daily one-to-one calls do not scale, make the project manager a bottleneck and leave no shared written record.",null],2],["pp14",0,1,"A project team grows from 5 people to 8 people. How many communication channels are added?",["3","13","18","28"],2,"Channels = n(n - 1) / 2. Five people have 5 x 4 / 2 = 10 channels; eight people have 8 x 7 / 2 = 28. The increase is 28 - 10 = 18. 28 is the new total, not the number added.","Domain 1.0: Communication management",["3 is just the number of people added, not the number of new communication channels.","13 does not come from the channel formula; the correct increase is 28 minus 10.",null,"28 is the total number of channels for 8 people, not the number added to the original 10."],3],["pp15",0,1,"A department head has great influence over whether the project succeeds but shows little interest in its details. Using a power/interest grid, how should the project manager engage this person?",["Keep satisfied","Manage closely","Keep informed","Monitor with minimal effort"],0,"High power and low interest calls for keeping the stakeholder satisfied: give them enough to stay comfortable without overloading them. Manage closely is for high power and high interest; keep informed is for low power and high interest.","Domain 1.0: Stakeholder management",[null,"Manage closely is for stakeholders with both high power and high interest, which this person does not have.","Keep informed fits low-power, high-interest stakeholders; this person has high power and little interest in detail.","Minimal monitoring is for low-power, low-interest stakeholders; ignoring a powerful person is risky."],2],["pp16",0,1,"In a RACI chart, how many people should be marked Accountable for a single task?",["At least two, for backup","Everyone marked Responsible","Exactly one","Nobody; the whole team shares it"],2,"Each task should have exactly one Accountable person who owns the outcome and signs off. Several people can be Responsible for doing the work, Consulted or Informed.","Domain 1.0: Resource management",["Having two accountable people creates confusion about who owns the outcome; backups belong in the Responsible role.","Several people can be Responsible for doing the work, but only one person should be Accountable.",null,"If nobody is Accountable, the task has no owner, which is the problem a RACI chart is meant to prevent."],1],["pp17",0,1,"Two tasks on the critical path both need the only database administrator in the same week. The project manager delays one task until the administrator is free, and the project end date moves out. Which technique was used?",["Resource smoothing","Resource leveling","Fast-tracking","Crashing"],1,"Leveling resolves over-allocation even if that changes the end date. Smoothing only shifts work within available float and never moves the end date. Fast-tracking and crashing are schedule compression techniques.","Domain 1.0: Resource management",["Resource smoothing only works within float and never moves the end date, but here the end date moved.",null,"Fast-tracking overlaps activities to shorten the schedule; it does not resolve a resource conflict by delaying a task.","Crashing adds resources to shorten the schedule, the opposite of delaying work until a person is free."],2],["pp18",0,1,"A project manager wants to even out a tester's workload by shifting some tasks within their available float, without changing the project end date. Which technique fits?",["Resource leveling","Crashing","Rolling-wave planning","Resource smoothing"],3,"Smoothing adjusts when work happens only within float, so the critical path and end date stay the same. Leveling can extend the schedule, and crashing adds resources to shorten it.","Domain 1.0: Resource management",["Resource leveling may extend the end date, which the project manager wants to avoid here.","Crashing adds resources to shorten the schedule and increases cost; it does not even out one person's workload.","Rolling-wave planning is about planning detail over time, not balancing a person's workload within float.",null],3],["pp19",0,1,"Task A (3 days) is first. B (5 days) and C (2 days) both follow A. D (4 days) follows C. E (1 day) follows both B and D. What is the shortest possible project duration?",["9 days","10 days","11 days","15 days"],1,"There are two paths: A-B-E = 3 + 5 + 1 = 9 days, and A-C-D-E = 3 + 2 + 4 + 1 = 10 days. The longest path is the critical path and sets the minimum duration, so the answer is 10 days. 15 days would be the sum of every task, which ignores parallel work.","Domain 1.0: Schedule development",["9 days is the length of path A-B-E, which is shorter than the critical path.",null,"11 days does not match either path; A-B-E is 9 days and A-C-D-E is 10 days.","15 days adds up every task, ignoring that B runs in parallel with C and D."],3],["pp20",0,1,"Using the same network (A 3 days first; B 5 days and C 2 days after A; D 4 days after C; E 1 day after B and D), how much total float does task B have?",["0 days","1 day","2 days","5 days"],1,"B is on the path A-B-E (9 days), while the critical path A-C-D-E is 10 days. B can slip by 10 - 9 = 1 day without delaying the project. Tasks on the critical path have zero float.","Domain 1.0: Schedule development",["Zero float applies only to activities on the critical path, and B is on the shorter 9-day path.",null,"2 days would require B's path to be 8 days; it is 9 days against a 10-day critical path.","5 days is B's duration, not the amount of time it can slip without delaying the project."],3],["pp21",0,1,"The sponsor needs a project finished two weeks early, and the budget allows extra spending. The project manager adds contractors to critical path activities. Which technique is this?",["Fast-tracking","Resource smoothing","Crashing","Scope decomposition"],2,"Crashing shortens the schedule by adding resources to critical path work, which raises cost. Fast-tracking instead overlaps activities that were planned in sequence, which raises the risk of rework rather than cost.","Domain 1.0: Schedule development",["Fast-tracking overlaps activities rather than adding people, and its main cost is added risk, not extra spending.","Resource smoothing redistributes work within float and does not shorten the project.",null,"Decomposing scope breaks work into smaller pieces for planning; it does not shorten the schedule by itself."],2],["pp22",0,1,"To recover a delayed schedule without adding budget, the team starts coding some modules before the design review is finished, accepting a higher chance of rework. Which technique is this?",["Crashing","Resource leveling","Gold plating","Fast-tracking"],3,"Fast-tracking performs activities in parallel that were planned to run one after another, which saves time but increases risk. Crashing adds resources and cost, and gold plating is adding unrequested extras.","Domain 1.0: Schedule development",["Crashing adds resources and budget, but the scenario says no extra budget was used.","Resource leveling resolves over-allocation and can lengthen the schedule; it does not overlap design and coding.","Gold plating is adding unrequested features, not overlapping activities to recover time.",null],2],["pp23",0,1,"User acceptance testing cannot begin until system testing is complete. Which type of dependency is this?",["Start-to-start","Finish-to-start","Finish-to-finish","Start-to-finish"],1,"In a finish-to-start dependency, the successor cannot start until the predecessor finishes. It is the most common relationship in project schedules. Start-to-finish, where the successor cannot finish until the predecessor starts, is the rarest.","Domain 1.0: Schedule development",["Start-to-start would let UAT begin as soon as system testing begins, not after it finishes.",null,"Finish-to-finish links the finish dates of two activities, not when the successor can start.","Start-to-finish means the successor cannot finish until the predecessor starts, which is the reverse of this situation."],1],["pp24",0,1,"On a Scrum team, who is responsible for ordering the product backlog so the most valuable items are delivered first?",["Product owner","Scrum master","Project sponsor","Senior developer"],0,"The product owner owns the product backlog and decides its order to maximize value. The scrum master coaches the team and removes impediments but does not decide priorities.","Domain 1.0: Agile methodology",[null,"The scrum master coaches the team and removes impediments but does not decide backlog priority.","The sponsor funds the effort but does not order the product backlog in Scrum.","Senior developers estimate and build items; ordering the backlog is the product owner's accountability."],1],["pp25",0,1,"At the daily standup, a developer says she has been unable to get access to the test environment for two days. On a Scrum team, who is primarily responsible for helping remove this impediment?",["Scrum master","Product owner","Project sponsor","QA lead"],0,"Removing impediments that block the team is a core responsibility of the scrum master. The product owner manages the backlog and value, not day-to-day blockers.","Domain 1.0: Agile methodology",[null,"The product owner manages backlog priority and value, not day-to-day blockers such as environment access.","The sponsor may help with major organizational obstacles but is not responsible for the team's daily impediments.","A QA lead may be affected by the missing environment but is not the Scrum role that removes impediments."],2],["pp26",0,1,"At the end of each sprint, the team meets privately to discuss what went well in their way of working and what they will change next sprint. Which Scrum event is this?",["Sprint review","Sprint planning","Sprint retrospective","Backlog refinement"],2,"The retrospective looks at the team's process and agrees on improvements. The sprint review is where the increment is shown to stakeholders to gather feedback on the product.","Domain 1.0: Agile methodology",["The sprint review shows the product to stakeholders; it is not the team's private look at its process.","Sprint planning selects work for the coming sprint rather than reflecting on how the last one went.",null,"Backlog refinement clarifies and estimates upcoming items; it does not review the team's way of working."],1],["pp27",0,1,"At the end of a sprint, the team demonstrates the completed features to stakeholders and gathers feedback that may change the backlog. Which Scrum event is this?",["Daily standup","Sprint review","Sprint retrospective","Sprint planning"],1,"The sprint review inspects the increment with stakeholders and adapts the backlog. The retrospective, held after it, focuses on how the team works rather than on the product.","Domain 1.0: Agile methodology",["The daily standup is a short team check on progress and blockers, not a stakeholder demonstration.",null,"The retrospective focuses on the team's process and is held without a product demonstration to stakeholders.","Sprint planning happens at the start of a sprint to choose work, not at the end to show completed features."],1],["pp28",0,1,"A Scrum team completed 30, 34 and 26 story points in its last three sprints. About 150 points remain in the backlog. Approximately how many more sprints will the team need?",["3","5","6","10"],1,"Average velocity is (30 + 34 + 26) / 3 = 30 points per sprint. 150 / 30 = 5 sprints. Velocity is a planning aid based on the team's own history, not a target to compare across teams.","Domain 1.0: Agile methodology",["3 sprints would require about 50 points per sprint, well above the team's average of 30.",null,"6 sprints would assume a velocity of 25 points, lower than the team's actual average of 30.","10 sprints would assume only 15 points per sprint, half the team's demonstrated velocity."],3],["pp29",0,1,"An IT support team handles a continuous stream of requests of varying size and wants to make flow visible and stop people from juggling too many tasks at once. Which approach fits best?",["Scrum with fixed four-week sprints","Waterfall with a detailed baseline","PRINCE2 with management stages","Kanban with work-in-progress limits"],3,"Kanban visualizes work on a board and uses WIP limits to reduce multitasking, which suits continuous, unpredictable incoming work. Fixed sprints and heavy up-front baselines fit poorly when priorities arrive every day.","Domain 1.0: Agile methodology",["Fixed four-week sprints suit planned product work; a support queue with daily new priorities does not fit long sprint commitments.","A detailed waterfall baseline assumes stable, known work, not a continuous stream of unpredictable requests.","PRINCE2 management stages are for governing projects, not for visualizing and limiting ongoing support work.",null],2],["pp30",0,1,"A regulated project to replace a payroll system has fixed, well-understood requirements, and auditors require formal sign-off at the end of each stage. Which approach is most appropriate?",["Scrum","Kanban","Extreme programming","Waterfall (predictive)"],3,"A predictive approach suits stable, well-defined requirements and formal phase sign-offs. Agile methods shine when requirements are expected to change and value is delivered in small increments.","Domain 1.0: Other methodologies",["Scrum suits evolving requirements delivered in increments, not fixed requirements with formal stage sign-offs.","Kanban manages a continuous flow of work and has no built-in phase sign-offs for auditors.","Extreme programming is an agile method for rapidly changing software requirements, not fixed, regulated stages.",null],2],["pp31",0,1,"A process improvement project uses the phases Define, Measure, Analyze, Improve and Control to reduce defects in server builds. Which methodology is this?",["Six Sigma","Lean","PRINCE2","Kanban"],0,"DMAIC is the Six Sigma improvement cycle, which uses data to reduce variation and defects. Lean focuses on removing waste, and PRINCE2 is a process-based project management method built on stages and tolerances.","Domain 1.0: Other methodologies",[null,"Lean focuses on removing waste and uses tools such as value stream mapping, not the DMAIC cycle.","PRINCE2 is a process-based project method built on stages and tolerances, not a defect-reduction cycle.","Kanban visualizes work and limits WIP; it does not define the Define-Measure-Analyze-Improve-Control phases."],2],["pp32",0,2,"Which document formally authorizes a project and gives the project manager authority to use organizational resources?",["Business case","Scope statement","Project charter","Statement of work"],2,"The charter, signed by the sponsor, officially starts the project and names the project manager. The business case justifies the investment before approval, and the scope statement is a more detailed planning document.","Domain 2.0: Initiating",["The business case justifies the investment before approval; it does not formally authorize the project or the PM.","The scope statement is a detailed planning document created after the project is authorized.",null,"A statement of work describes work a vendor will perform; it does not authorize the whole project internally."],1],["pp33",0,2,"Before approving a new document management system, leadership wants to know whether the expected benefits justify the cost and which options were considered. Which document answers this?",["Project charter","Business case","Communication plan","RACI chart"],1,"The business case explains the problem or opportunity, compares options, and shows costs, benefits and return so leaders can decide whether to fund the project. The charter comes after that decision.","Domain 2.0: Discovery and concept preparation",["The charter comes after the funding decision and authorizes the project; it is not the main cost-benefit justification.",null,"The communication plan describes how information is shared, not whether the investment is worthwhile.","A RACI chart assigns responsibilities and says nothing about costs, benefits or options."],2],["pp34",0,2,"A project will cost $120,000 and is expected to save $40,000 per year. What is the payback period?",["3 years","18 months","2 years","4 years"],0,"Payback period = initial investment / annual savings = $120,000 / $40,000 = 3 years. A shorter payback is generally more attractive, though payback ignores benefits after the break-even point.","Domain 2.0: Discovery and concept preparation",[null,"18 months would require savings of $80,000 per year, double the stated amount.","2 years would require $60,000 in annual savings, not the $40,000 given.","4 years would mean savings of only $30,000 per year, less than the stated amount."],2],["pp35",0,2,"During discovery, the team checks whether the existing office network has enough capacity and quality of service to support a proposed VoIP system. What activity is this?",["Lessons learned review","Quality audit","Procurement closure","Feasibility study"],3,"A feasibility study checks whether a proposed project is technically, financially and operationally possible before committing to it. Audits and lessons learned happen once work is under way or finished.","Domain 2.0: Discovery and concept preparation",["A lessons learned review captures insights from work already done, not whether a proposed system is viable.","A quality audit checks whether processes are being followed during execution, not whether a project is possible.","Procurement closure finishes vendor contracts at the end of a project, not an early viability check.",null],2],["pp36",0,2,"The charter has been signed and key stakeholders have been identified. The project manager now wants to align everyone on objectives, roles, communication and next steps. Which meeting should be held?",["Phase gate review","Sprint retrospective","Kickoff meeting","Closing meeting"],2,"The kickoff meeting launches the project with stakeholders and the team, setting shared expectations about goals, roles and how the project will run. A phase gate review decides whether to continue at the end of a phase.","Domain 2.0: Initiating",["A phase gate review decides whether to continue at the end of a phase, not how to launch the project.","A sprint retrospective is a Scrum event for improving the team's process, not a project launch meeting.",null,"A closing meeting marks the end of the project, not the start of work after the charter is signed."],1],["pp37",0,2,"The project charter states, \"The vendor will deliver all laptops within 30 days of the order.\" This statement has not been confirmed. How should it be classified?",["Constraint","Assumption","Requirement","Milestone"],1,"An assumption is something believed to be true for planning purposes but not proven. It should be tracked, validated and treated as a possible risk. A constraint is a fixed limit, such as a mandated deadline or budget cap.","Domain 2.0: Initiating",["A constraint is a fixed limit such as a deadline or budget cap; an unconfirmed expectation about delivery is not a limit.",null,"A requirement describes what the product must do, not an unverified belief about a vendor's delivery time.","A milestone is a significant point in the schedule, not a statement about what the team believes will happen."],2],["pp38",0,2,"A compliance project must be finished before a regulatory deadline of March 31, and the date cannot move. How should this be recorded?",["Assumption","Risk","Dependency","Constraint"],3,"A constraint is a limitation the project must work within, such as a fixed deadline, budget or resource. An assumption is an unproven belief, and a risk is an uncertain event.","Domain 2.0: Initiating",["An assumption is an unproven belief; this date is a known, fixed limit.","A risk is an uncertain event; the deadline is certain and imposed on the project.","A dependency is a relationship between activities, not an externally imposed finish date.",null],2],["pp39",0,2,"The project manager breaks the deliverable \"new office network\" into cabling, switching, wireless and testing, then breaks each of those into smaller work packages. What is being created?",["Gantt chart","Network diagram","Work breakdown structure","Resource calendar"],2,"A WBS decomposes the total scope into deliverables and work packages. A Gantt chart shows scheduled activities over time, and a network diagram shows the sequence and dependencies between activities.","Domain 2.0: Planning",["A Gantt chart shows scheduled activities over time, not the decomposition of a deliverable into work packages.","A network diagram shows the sequence and dependencies of activities, not the breakdown of scope.",null,"A resource calendar shows when people and equipment are available, not how the scope breaks down."],1],["pp40",0,2,"A team member needs the detailed description, acceptance criteria, owner and estimate for one specific work package. Where should they look?",["Project charter","WBS dictionary","Issue log","Stakeholder register"],1,"The WBS dictionary gives detailed information about each WBS element. The charter is high level, the issue log tracks current problems, and the stakeholder register lists people and their interests.","Domain 2.0: Planning",["The charter is high level and does not describe individual work packages in detail.",null,"The issue log tracks current problems, not the description and acceptance criteria of work packages.","The stakeholder register lists people and their interests, not details of specific work packages."],2],["pp41",0,2,"A project manager estimates an email migration by using the actual duration of a similar migration the company completed last year. Which estimating technique is this?",["Analogous","Parametric","Bottom-up","Three-point"],0,"Analogous (top-down) estimating uses a similar past project as the basis. It is fast but less accurate. Parametric uses a unit rate times a quantity; bottom-up adds up work package estimates.","Domain 2.0: Planning",[null,"Parametric estimating multiplies a unit rate by a quantity rather than reusing a whole past project's duration.","Bottom-up estimating adds up detailed work package estimates instead of comparing with a past project.","Three-point estimating uses optimistic, most likely and pessimistic values, not one similar past project."],2],["pp42",0,2,"Imaging one workstation takes 2 hours. The project manager estimates that imaging 150 workstations will take 300 hours. Which estimating technique is this?",["Analogous estimating","Parametric estimating","Three-point estimating","Rolling-wave planning"],1,"Parametric estimating multiplies a known unit rate by the quantity of work (2 hours x 150). Analogous estimating compares the whole job to a past project instead.","Domain 2.0: Planning",["Analogous estimating compares the whole job to a similar past project instead of using a unit rate.",null,"Three-point estimating uses optimistic, most likely and pessimistic values, not a rate multiplied by quantity.","Rolling-wave planning is about adding planning detail over time, not an estimating formula."],2],["pp43",0,2,"For a server build, the optimistic estimate is 4 days, the most likely is 6 days and the pessimistic is 14 days. Using the PERT (beta) formula, what is the expected duration?",["6 days","7 days","8 days","9 days"],1,"PERT expected duration = (O + 4M + P) / 6 = (4 + 24 + 14) / 6 = 42 / 6 = 7 days. A simple triangular average would give (4 + 6 + 14) / 3 = 8 days.","Domain 2.0: Planning",["6 days is the most likely value alone, ignoring the weighting of the optimistic and pessimistic estimates.",null,"8 days is the simple triangular average (O + M + P) / 3, not the PERT beta formula.","9 days does not match either formula for these estimates."],3],["pp44",0,2,"The sponsor wants the most accurate cost estimate possible and is willing to wait while the team estimates every work package and adds them up. Which technique is this?",["Bottom-up","Analogous","Top-down","Parametric"],0,"Bottom-up estimating builds the total from detailed work package estimates. It takes the most time but is usually the most accurate. Analogous is the same as top-down and is the least detailed.","Domain 2.0: Planning",[null,"Analogous estimating is quick and based on a past project; it is the least detailed, not the most accurate.","Top-down is another name for analogous estimating, which is faster but less accurate than bottom-up.","Parametric estimating uses a unit rate and quantity; it does not estimate and add up every work package."],2],["pp45",0,2,"What is the approved version of the scope, schedule and cost that is used to measure project performance?",["Forecast","Backlog","Burndown","Baseline"],3,"Baselines are the approved plans that actual results are compared against. They change only through formal change control. A forecast is a prediction of future results based on current performance.","Domain 2.0: Planning",["A forecast predicts future results from current performance; it is not the approved plan used for comparison.","A backlog is a prioritized list of work items, mainly in agile, not an approved scope, schedule and cost plan.","A burndown chart shows remaining work over time; it is a reporting tool, not the approved plan.",null],1],["pp46",0,2,"An organization is buying a well-defined, fully specified network cabling job and wants the vendor to carry the risk of cost overruns. Which contract type fits best?",["Time and materials","Cost plus fixed fee","Firm fixed price","Cost plus award fee"],2,"Under a firm fixed price contract, the seller agrees to deliver the defined scope for a set price, so the seller carries cost risk. Cost-reimbursable contracts put more risk on the buyer.","Domain 2.0: Procurement",["Time and materials pays for hours and materials used, so the buyer, not the vendor, carries the risk of overruns.","Cost plus fixed fee reimburses the vendor's actual costs, leaving the buyer exposed to overruns.",null,"Cost plus award fee also reimburses actual costs, so the buyer carries most of the cost risk."],2],["pp47",0,2,"A project needs two extra developers for an uncertain amount of time while requirements are still being worked out. Which contract type is most suitable?",["Firm fixed price","Time and materials","Fixed price with economic price adjustment","Single purchase order for goods"],1,"Time and materials contracts pay for hours worked and materials used, which suits staff augmentation and unclear scope. A fixed price needs a well-defined scope to be fair to both sides.","Domain 2.0: Procurement",["A firm fixed price needs a well-defined scope; with unclear requirements and duration it would be hard to price fairly.",null,"A fixed price with economic adjustment still requires a defined scope; it only adjusts for inflation or market changes.","A purchase order for goods is used to buy defined items, not to pay for developers' time on uncertain work."],2],["pp48",0,2,"A company wants vendors to describe how they would solve a help desk problem, including their approach, timeline and price. Which procurement document should it issue?",["Request for information (RFI)","Request for quote (RFQ)","Purchase order (PO)","Request for proposal (RFP)"],3,"An RFP asks vendors for a proposed solution and price when the buyer knows the need but not the best approach. An RFI only gathers general information, and an RFQ asks for prices on something already specified.","Domain 2.0: Procurement",["An RFI only gathers general market information and does not ask vendors for a proposed solution with price.","An RFQ asks for prices on something already fully specified, but here the approach is still open.","A purchase order commits to buy after a vendor is chosen; it does not invite proposals.",null],2],["pp49",0,2,"The team knows exactly which laptop model and configuration it needs and only wants prices for 300 units from several suppliers. Which document should be issued?",["Request for quote (RFQ)","Request for proposal (RFP)","Request for information (RFI)","Statement of work (SOW)"],0,"An RFQ is used when requirements are fully defined and price is the main deciding factor. An RFP asks vendors to propose a solution, and an RFI is for early market research.","Domain 2.0: Procurement",[null,"An RFP asks vendors to propose a solution, which is unnecessary when the exact model is already chosen.","An RFI is for early market research, not for collecting prices on a fully specified item.","A statement of work describes work a vendor will perform, not a request for prices on products."],1],["pp50",0,2,"Which of these activities is quality assurance rather than quality control?",["Testing a finished build against the acceptance criteria","Counting defects found during user acceptance testing","Auditing whether the team follows its test process","Inspecting a new cable run with a certifier"],2,"Quality assurance focuses on processes: making sure the right methods are followed so defects are prevented. Testing, inspecting and counting defects in the deliverables themselves are quality control activities.","Domain 2.0: Executing",["Testing a finished build checks the product itself, which makes it a quality control activity.","Counting defects found in testing measures the product's quality, which is quality control.",null,"Inspecting a cable run with a certifier checks a deliverable, which is quality control, not process assurance."],3],["pp51",0,2,"A deliverable has passed all internal testing. What must happen before it is considered complete?",["The project manager closes the project and releases the team","The team starts the next phase without a review of the deliverable","The customer formally accepts it against the acceptance criteria","The vendor issues the final invoice for the project"],2,"Internal testing is quality control, but a deliverable is complete only when the customer or sponsor accepts it against agreed acceptance criteria, usually with a signed acceptance form.","Domain 2.0: Executing",["The project cannot close before the deliverable is formally accepted; closing comes after acceptance.","Moving on without reviewing the deliverable skips acceptance and risks building on work the customer rejects.",null,"A final invoice is a vendor billing step, not the customer's confirmation that the deliverable meets its criteria."],2],["pp52",0,2,"Status reports show the team is three weeks behind the baseline for a key milestone. What should the project manager do first?",["Analyze the cause of the variance and evaluate corrective actions","Rebaseline the schedule so that it matches the current actual progress","Remove the milestone from the schedule and inform the sponsor later","Tell the team to work overtime every weekend until the end of the project"],0,"Monitoring and controlling starts with understanding why a variance happened, then choosing corrective action. Rebaselining requires approved change control and should not be used to hide poor performance.","Domain 2.0: Monitoring and controlling",[null,"Rebaselining to match actual progress hides the variance and requires approved change control.","Removing a milestone and informing the sponsor later hides the problem and breaks change control.","Indefinite overtime is a reaction without knowing the cause, and it burns out the team."],2],["pp53",0,2,"Fixing a critical issue will require more money than the project manager is authorized to spend under the agreed tolerances. What should the project manager do?",["Take the money from another project's budget without approval","Escalate to the sponsor through the escalation path","Record the issue in lessons learned and carry on as planned","Ask the vendor to absorb the extra cost without a contract change"],1,"When a decision exceeds the project manager's authority or tolerance, it is escalated using the path defined in the plan, usually to the sponsor or steering committee. Moving funds without approval breaks governance.","Domain 2.0: Monitoring and controlling",["Moving money from another project without approval breaks governance and exceeds the project manager's authority.",null,"Recording it in lessons learned and carrying on leaves a critical issue unresolved.","A vendor has no obligation to absorb costs outside the contract; that would need a negotiated change order."],2],["pp54",0,2,"All deliverables have been accepted by the customer. Before releasing the team, what should the project manager do?",["Start the kickoff meeting for the next project with the same team","Submit new change requests for features that were cut from scope","Recalculate the business case using the original estimates","Hold a lessons learned session and archive the project documents"],3,"Closing includes capturing lessons learned while the team is still available and archiving records for future projects and audits. Once the team is released, it is much harder to collect their insights.","Domain 2.0: Closing",["Starting another project's kickoff before closing this one leaves lessons and records uncaptured.","Cut features would need new change requests or a future project, not activity during closing.","Recalculating the business case with original estimates adds nothing at closing; actual results are compared instead.",null],2],["pp55",0,2,"A new ticketing system is about to go live. Which closing activity makes sure the service desk can run and support it after the project team leaves?",["Contract closure with each of the vendors","Transition to operations","Final reconciliation of the project budget","Releasing project resources to other work"],1,"Transition (handoff) to operations passes the product to the people who will support it, with documentation, training, support procedures and agreed acceptance. The other activities are also closing tasks but do not prepare the support team.","Domain 2.0: Closing",["Contract closure settles vendor obligations and payments; it does not prepare the service desk to support the system.",null,"Budget reconciliation confirms final costs but does not give the support team knowledge or procedures.","Releasing resources frees the project team; without a handoff first, nobody is prepared to support the system."],2],["pp56",0,2,"Before closing the project, the project manager confirms that the vendor delivered everything in the contract, the final payment has been made and procurement records have been archived. Which activity is this?",["Contract closure","Scope validation","Quality control","Risk reassessment"],0,"Contract (procurement) closure confirms that all contractual obligations are met, settles open claims and payments, and archives procurement records. Scope validation is the customer's formal acceptance of deliverables.","Domain 2.0: Closing",[null,"Scope validation is the customer's formal acceptance of deliverables, not settling vendor obligations and payments.","Quality control inspects and tests deliverables for defects; it does not close out the vendor contract.","Reassessing risks is a monitoring activity, not the confirmation and archiving of procurement records."],2],["pp57",0,2,"The sponsor cancels a project halfway through because of a change in company strategy. What should the project manager do?",["Stop at once and delete the project workspace to save costs","Keep working until the remaining budget has been spent","Hand the project to another manager without any documentation","Follow the closing process for the work done so far"],3,"Cancelled projects still need orderly closure: document what was completed, capture lessons learned, close contracts, release resources and archive records. Deleting files or continuing work wastes value and breaks governance.","Domain 2.0: Closing",["Deleting the workspace destroys records needed for audits and lessons learned and skips contract closure.","Continuing work after cancellation wastes money on a project the sponsor no longer supports.","Handing over without documentation loses what was learned and completed, and the project still needs to close.",null],2],["pp58",0,2,"A project needs requirements from 200 users spread across 12 countries, and there is limited time. Which technique is most efficient?",["One-on-one interviews with each of the 200 users","Job shadowing every user for a full working day","An online survey or questionnaire","A single focus group of five users at headquarters"],2,"Surveys collect input from many people quickly and across locations. Interviews and shadowing give depth but cannot scale to 200 users in a short time, and one small focus group would not represent the whole population.","Domain 2.0: Planning",["Interviewing 200 people one by one would take far longer than the limited time allows.","Shadowing every user for a full day is extremely time-consuming and impractical across 12 countries.",null,"Five users at headquarters do not represent 200 users across 12 countries."],3],["pp59",0,3,"A project manager needs a visual that shows each task as a bar against a calendar, along with dependencies and milestones. Which tool should be used?",["Pareto chart","Gantt chart","Scatter diagram","Histogram"],1,"A Gantt chart shows tasks as horizontal bars on a timeline, with start and end dates, dependencies and milestones. Pareto charts, scatter diagrams and histograms are quality analysis tools.","Domain 3.0: Project management tools",["A Pareto chart ranks categories by frequency; it does not show tasks against a calendar.",null,"A scatter diagram shows the relationship between two variables, not scheduled tasks and milestones.","A histogram shows how often values fall into ranges, not tasks on a timeline."],1],["pp60",0,3,"The service desk wants to find the few ticket categories that cause most of its incidents so a project can target them. Which chart is most useful?",["Run chart","Burnup chart","Gantt chart","Pareto chart"],3,"A Pareto chart sorts categories from most to least frequent with a cumulative percentage line, showing the vital few causes (the 80/20 principle). A run chart shows one measure over time.","Domain 3.0: Quality charts",["A run chart shows one measure over time but does not rank categories to reveal the vital few.","A burnup chart shows completed work against total scope, not which ticket categories cause the most incidents.","A Gantt chart shows scheduled tasks and has nothing to do with ranking incident causes.",null],2],["pp61",0,3,"Deployments keep failing, and the team wants to brainstorm possible causes grouped into categories such as people, process, tools and environment. Which tool fits best?",["Burndown chart","Histogram","Fishbone (Ishikawa) diagram","RACI chart"],2,"A fishbone diagram organizes possible causes of one problem into categories along the \"bones\", which supports root cause analysis. A histogram shows how often values fall into ranges.","Domain 3.0: Quality charts",["A burndown chart shows remaining work over time, not possible causes of a problem.","A histogram shows how often values occur; it does not organize possible causes into categories.",null,"A RACI chart assigns responsibilities and does not support root cause brainstorming."],2],["pp62",0,3,"A performance tester wants to see whether page response time goes up as the number of concurrent users increases. Which chart should be used?",["Scatter diagram","Pareto chart","Gantt chart","Kanban board"],0,"A scatter diagram plots pairs of values for two variables to show whether they are correlated. Pareto charts rank categories, and Gantt charts and Kanban boards show work, not relationships between variables.","Domain 3.0: Quality charts",[null,"A Pareto chart ranks categories by frequency; it does not test whether two measures move together.","A Gantt chart shows tasks over time, not the relationship between response time and user count.","A Kanban board tracks work items through a workflow, not correlations in performance data."],2],["pp63",0,3,"A quality lead wants to know whether the time taken to provision new user accounts stays within upper and lower limits over several months. Which chart is designed for this?",["Fishbone diagram","Burnup chart","Control chart","Pareto chart"],2,"A control chart plots a process measure over time against a mean and upper and lower control limits, showing whether the process is stable. Points outside the limits signal a problem to investigate.","Domain 3.0: Quality charts",["A fishbone diagram helps brainstorm causes but does not track a measure over time against limits.","A burnup chart tracks completed work and scope, not whether a process measure stays within limits.",null,"A Pareto chart ranks categories and has no control limits or time axis."],3],["pp64",0,3,"Midway through a sprint, the actual line on the burndown chart is well above the ideal line. What does this indicate?",["More work remains than planned at this point","The team is ahead of its sprint plan","Scope was removed from the sprint","The team's velocity has doubled"],0,"A burndown chart tracks remaining work. If the actual line is above the ideal line, more work remains than expected, so the team is behind. A line below the ideal line would mean it is ahead.","Domain 3.0: Agile reporting",[null,"Being ahead would put the actual line below the ideal line, not above it.","Removing scope would pull the remaining-work line down, not keep it above the ideal line.","A doubled velocity would bring the line down faster; the chart shows the opposite."],2],["pp65",0,3,"Stakeholders keep adding items to a release, and the product owner wants one chart that shows both completed work and changes to total scope over time. Which chart is best?",["Burndown chart","Burnup chart","Histogram","Fishbone diagram"],1,"A burnup chart has a completed-work line and a separate total-scope line, so scope growth is visible when the scope line rises. A burndown chart mixes scope changes into one remaining-work line, which hides them.","Domain 3.0: Agile reporting",["A burndown chart combines scope changes into one remaining-work line, which hides how much scope has grown.",null,"A histogram shows a distribution of values and has no time-based view of work and scope.","A fishbone diagram explores causes of a problem and does not track progress or scope."],3],["pp66",0,3,"A project has earned value (EV) of $80,000 and actual cost (AC) of $100,000. What does this show?",["CPI is 1.25, so the project is under budget","CPI is 1.0, so the project is on budget","SPI is 0.8, so the project is behind schedule","CPI is 0.8, so the project is over budget"],3,"Cost performance index = EV / AC = 80,000 / 100,000 = 0.8. Below 1 means the project is getting 80 cents of value for each dollar spent: over budget. SPI would need planned value, which is not given.","Domain 3.0: Earned value management",["1.25 is AC divided by EV, the inverted formula; CPI is EV divided by AC.","A CPI of 1.0 would require EV to equal AC, but the work cost $20,000 more than its earned value.","SPI needs planned value, which is not given, so no schedule conclusion can be drawn.",null],3],["pp67",0,3,"At a checkpoint, planned value (PV) is $100,000 and earned value (EV) is $90,000. How is the project doing on schedule?",["SPI is 1.1; the project is ahead of schedule","CPI is 0.9; the project is over budget","SPI is 0.9; the project is behind schedule","SV is +$10,000; the project is ahead of schedule"],2,"Schedule performance index = EV / PV = 90,000 / 100,000 = 0.9, and schedule variance = EV - PV = -$10,000. Both show less work done than planned. Cost cannot be judged without actual cost.","Domain 3.0: Earned value management",["1.1 inverts the formula; SPI is EV divided by PV, which is 0.9.","CPI requires actual cost, which is not given, so no budget conclusion can be drawn.",null,"Schedule variance is EV minus PV, which is -$10,000, meaning behind schedule, not ahead."],3],["pp68",0,3,"A project has earned value of $50,000 and actual cost of $60,000. What is the cost variance?",["+$10,000","-$10,000","-$5,000","+$110,000"],1,"Cost variance = EV - AC = 50,000 - 60,000 = -$10,000. A negative cost variance means the project is over budget for the work completed.","Domain 3.0: Earned value management",["+$10,000 reverses the formula; cost variance is EV minus AC, so it is negative here.",null,"-$5,000 does not follow from the numbers; the difference between EV and AC is $10,000.","+$110,000 adds EV and AC together, which is not how cost variance is calculated."],2],["pp69",0,3,"A project's budget at completion (BAC) is $200,000 and its CPI is 0.8. If the current cost performance continues, what is the estimate at completion (EAC)?",["$160,000","$200,000","$240,000","$250,000"],3,"EAC = BAC / CPI = 200,000 / 0.8 = $250,000. Multiplying by CPI instead ($160,000) is a common mistake; when CPI is below 1, the forecast must be higher than the budget.","Domain 3.0: Earned value management",["$160,000 multiplies BAC by CPI instead of dividing, which wrongly forecasts a cost below budget.","$200,000 is the original budget and ignores the poor cost performance shown by the CPI.","$240,000 does not follow from BAC divided by CPI; it understates the forecast.",null],3],["pp70",0,3,"An auditor asks to see every change request submitted on the project, along with its status and the decision made. Which document should the project manager provide?",["Change log","Risk register","Issue log","Stakeholder register"],0,"The change log records each change request, its status (submitted, approved, rejected, deferred) and the decision. The issue log tracks current problems, and the risk register tracks uncertain future events.","Domain 3.0: Project documents",[null,"The risk register tracks uncertain future events, not the history of change requests and decisions.","The issue log tracks current problems; it is not the record of change requests and their status.","The stakeholder register lists people and their interests, not change requests."],1],["pp71",0,3,"After a steering meeting, the project manager sends out a record of the decisions made, the action items, their owners and due dates. What is this document?",["Meeting agenda","Project charter","Scope statement","Meeting minutes"],3,"Minutes record what was decided and who will do what by when. The agenda is sent before a meeting to list the topics and goals.","Domain 3.0: Meeting documentation",["An agenda is sent before a meeting to list topics; it does not record decisions and action items afterward.","The project charter authorizes the project and is not a record of a single meeting.","A scope statement describes deliverables and exclusions, not what was decided in a meeting.",null],1],["pp72",0,3,"Senior executives want a single page they can glance at to see overall project health, key milestones and top risks with red, amber and green indicators. What should the project manager provide?",["A dashboard or executive summary","The full WBS dictionary with all entries","An export of the complete issue log","The team's detailed sprint task list"],0,"Executives need concise, visual summaries such as a dashboard with RAG status. Detailed working documents are useful to the team but overwhelm a senior audience; tailor the report to the audience.","Domain 3.0: Status reporting",[null,"The full WBS dictionary is a detailed working document that would overwhelm executives who want a quick view.","A complete issue log export is raw detail; executives need a summary of top issues and overall health.","A detailed sprint task list is useful to the team but far too granular for a one-page executive view."],1],["pp73",0,3,"Before sharing proprietary network designs with a potential vendor during evaluation, what should the organization have the vendor sign?",["Service level agreement (SLA)","Memorandum of understanding (MOU)","Purchase order (PO)","Non-disclosure agreement (NDA)"],3,"An NDA legally binds the vendor to keep shared information confidential. An SLA sets service levels, an MOU records a non-binding intent to work together, and a PO is a formal order to buy.","Domain 3.0: Vendor documents",["An SLA sets measurable service levels for an ongoing service; it does not protect confidential designs.","An MOU states an intent to cooperate and is usually not a binding confidentiality agreement.","A purchase order commits to buying goods or services; it gives no confidentiality protection.",null],1],["pp74",0,3,"A hosting contract states that the provider guarantees 99.9% monthly availability and a four-hour response time for critical incidents, with service credits if missed. Which document contains these terms?",["Statement of work (SOW)","Service level agreement (SLA)","Non-disclosure agreement (NDA)","Request for information (RFI)"],1,"An SLA defines measurable service levels and the remedies if they are not met. A statement of work describes the work and deliverables of a project, not ongoing service targets.","Domain 3.0: Vendor documents",["A statement of work describes project work and deliverables, not ongoing availability targets and service credits.",null,"An NDA protects confidential information and does not define uptime or response times.","An RFI is a pre-purchase request for market information, not a contract with service guarantees."],1],["pp75",0,3,"A risk has a 20% probability of occurring and would cost $50,000 if it did. What is its expected monetary value?",["$5,000","$10,000","$25,000","$40,000"],1,"Expected monetary value = probability x impact = 0.20 x $50,000 = $10,000. EMV is used in decision trees and quantitative risk analysis to compare options and size contingency reserves.","Domain 3.0: Decision-making tools",["$5,000 would be a 10% probability; the risk has a 20% chance.",null,"$25,000 would assume a 50% probability, not the stated 20%.","$40,000 is the impact minus the EMV, not probability multiplied by impact."],2],["pp76",0,4,"A project will move patient treatment records from a clinic's old system to a new cloud application. Which type of data is involved, and which U.S. regulation most directly applies?",["PCI data, governed by PCI DSS","Public data, with no regulation","PHI, governed by HIPAA","Financial data, governed by SOX"],2,"Patient treatment records are protected health information (PHI), and in the United States HIPAA governs how they are protected. PCI DSS applies to payment card data.","Domain 4.0: Data privacy and compliance",["Treatment records are health data, not payment card data, so PCI DSS is not the governing rule here.","Patient records are highly sensitive and regulated; treating them as public data would be a serious error.",null,"SOX concerns financial reporting controls at public companies, not the privacy of patient treatment records."],2],["pp77",0,4,"An online store project will add a checkout that stores and processes customers' credit card numbers. Which standard must the design meet?",["HIPAA","FERPA","ISO 9001","PCI DSS"],3,"The Payment Card Industry Data Security Standard applies to any organization that stores, processes or transmits cardholder data. HIPAA covers health information, FERPA covers student records, and ISO 9001 is a quality management standard.","Domain 4.0: Data privacy and compliance",["HIPAA protects health information, not customers' payment card numbers.","FERPA protects student education records, not card data in an online store.","ISO 9001 is a quality management standard and does not set requirements for protecting cardholder data.",null],1],["pp78",0,4,"A U.S. company's marketing project will collect personal data from residents of Germany and France. Which regulation must the project account for?",["HIPAA","GDPR","PCI DSS","FISMA"],1,"The EU General Data Protection Regulation applies to organizations that process personal data of people in the EU, wherever the organization is based. It affects consent, data minimization, rights of access and deletion, and breach notification.","Domain 4.0: Data privacy and compliance",["HIPAA governs U.S. health information, not general marketing data about EU residents.",null,"PCI DSS applies to payment card data, not personal data collected for marketing.","FISMA applies to U.S. federal information systems, not a private company's marketing data about EU residents."],1],["pp79",0,4,"A contractor on a data migration project needs to read one database for four weeks. What access should the project manager request?",["Administrator rights to all servers so work is never blocked","A shared team account that everyone on the project uses","Read-only access to that database for the contract period","The same access as the database administrator, with no end date"],2,"Least privilege means granting only the access needed for the task, for only as long as it is needed. Shared accounts remove accountability, and broad or permanent rights increase the damage if the account is misused.","Domain 4.0: Security concepts",["Administrator rights to all servers far exceed what the task needs and violate least privilege.","A shared account removes individual accountability and cannot be revoked for one person.",null,"Permanent administrator-level access exceeds both the scope and the duration of the contractor's need."],2],["pp80",0,4,"A server upgrade will make the payroll application unavailable for two hours. How should the deployment be scheduled?",["During an approved maintenance window, with users notified","During business hours, so the team is available to help users","Whenever the engineer finishes preparing the new server","At any time, because two hours of downtime is minor"],0,"Planned downtime should fall in an approved maintenance window, usually at low-use times, with affected users notified in advance. Unannounced outages during business hours disrupt operations.","Domain 4.0: Operational change management",[null,"Business hours are when downtime causes the most disruption, which is why maintenance windows exist.","Deploying whenever the engineer is ready ignores change management and gives users no warning.","Two hours without payroll matters to the business; downtime must be approved and communicated, not assumed to be minor."],1],["pp81",0,4,"A retail company imposes a change freeze from mid-November through early January. A project wants to deploy a non-urgent upgrade to the point-of-sale system in December. What should happen?",["Reschedule the deployment for after the freeze","Deploy it anyway, because the project plan was approved earlier","Deploy it at night, since freezes only apply during business hours","Classify it as an emergency change so it can bypass the freeze"],0,"A change freeze blocks non-emergency changes during critical business periods. The project must plan around it. Mislabeling a routine change as an emergency undermines change management.","Domain 4.0: Operational change management",[null,"An earlier plan approval does not override a change freeze set to protect critical business periods.","Freezes cover the whole period, not only business hours, so deploying at night still breaks the freeze.","Mislabeling a routine upgrade as an emergency abuses the expedited process and undermines change management."],2],["pp82",0,4,"What must a production change request include in case the deployment fails?",["A lessons learned report","A procurement statement of work","A rollback (backout) plan","A stakeholder power/interest grid"],2,"A rollback plan describes how to restore the previous working state if the change fails, and the change advisory board expects it before approval. Lessons learned come after the fact.","Domain 4.0: Operational change management",["A lessons learned report is written after the fact and does not restore service if the deployment fails.","A statement of work describes vendor work and does not tell operators how to recover from a failed change.",null,"A power/interest grid is a stakeholder analysis tool with no role in recovering from a failed deployment."],1],["pp83",0,4,"Which group typically reviews and approves changes to production IT systems, weighing risk, impact and scheduling across the whole organization?",["Project management office (PMO)","Change advisory board (CAB)","Steering committee for the project","Scrum team for the product"],1,"The CAB evaluates operational changes to production services for risk, conflicts and timing. The PMO sets project standards, and a project's steering committee oversees that one project.","Domain 4.0: Operational change management",["The PMO sets project standards and governance, but it does not approve changes to production systems.",null,"A project's steering committee oversees one project, not changes across the whole production environment.","A Scrum team builds the product; it does not approve organization-wide production changes."],2],["pp84",0,4,"A company wants a new email and calendaring service but does not want to manage servers, operating systems or application updates. Which cloud service model fits?",["Infrastructure as a service (IaaS)","Platform as a service (PaaS)","On-premises virtualization","Software as a service (SaaS)"],3,"With SaaS, the provider runs the whole application; the customer configures it and manages users and data. IaaS still leaves the OS and applications to the customer, and PaaS leaves the application code.","Domain 4.0: Cloud models",["IaaS would leave the company managing operating systems and application updates, which it wants to avoid.","PaaS still requires the company to build and maintain its own application code.","On-premises virtualization keeps servers, operating systems and updates in-house, the opposite of what is wanted.",null],2],["pp85",0,4,"A development team needs full control of the operating system and installed software for a legacy application but does not want to buy or maintain physical hardware. Which cloud model fits?",["Software as a service (SaaS)","Platform as a service (PaaS)","Infrastructure as a service (IaaS)","Function as a service (FaaS)"],2,"IaaS provides virtual machines, storage and networking while the customer manages the OS and everything above it. PaaS and FaaS hide the OS, which would prevent the needed control.","Domain 4.0: Cloud models",["SaaS hides the OS and software entirely, so the team could not control the operating system.","PaaS manages the OS for the customer, which prevents the full OS control the legacy app needs.",null,"Function as a service runs small pieces of code without any OS access, which does not suit a legacy application."],2],["pp86",0,4,"Where should business users perform user acceptance testing of a new release before it goes live?",["In a staging or test environment that mirrors production","Directly in production during business hours","On the developers' own workstations","In the disaster recovery site's live systems"],0,"UAT belongs in a non-production environment that closely matches production, so real issues surface without affecting live users or data. Testing in production risks outages and data corruption.","Domain 4.0: SDLC and environments",[null,"Testing first in production risks outages and data problems for live users during business hours.","Developers' workstations do not reflect production and are for coding and unit testing, not business UAT.","Live disaster recovery systems must stay ready for failover; they are not a test environment."],1],["pp87",0,4,"Which DevOps practice automatically builds the code and runs tests every time a developer commits changes to the shared repository?",["Change freeze","Continuous integration","Waterfall release","Manual regression testing"],1,"Continuous integration (CI) merges changes frequently and runs automated builds and tests, catching problems early. Continuous delivery or deployment (CD) then moves tested builds toward production.","Domain 4.0: SDLC and DevOps",["A change freeze blocks changes during critical periods; it does not build or test code.",null,"A waterfall release delivers software at the end of sequential phases rather than testing every commit automatically.","Manual regression testing is done by people periodically, not automatically on every commit."],2],["pp88",0,4,"The business states that the order system can lose no more than one hour of data after a failure. Which metric does this define, and what does it drive?",["RTO; it drives how quickly the service must be restored","MTBF; it drives how often hardware is replaced","SLA; it drives the vendor's monthly service credits","RPO; it drives how often backups or replication occur"],3,"The recovery point objective is the maximum acceptable data loss measured in time, so it determines backup or replication frequency. The recovery time objective is the maximum acceptable downtime.","Domain 4.0: Business continuity",["RTO is maximum acceptable downtime, not how much data can be lost.","MTBF measures average time between failures and does not state tolerable data loss.","An SLA is an agreement with a provider, not the business metric for acceptable data loss.",null],3],["pp89",0,4,"A project is decommissioning old file servers whose drives held confidential customer data. What should happen to the drives?",["Sanitize or destroy them and keep a certificate of destruction","Sell them as they are to recover some of the project budget","Delete the files and reformat the drives, then donate them","Store them in an unlocked cupboard in case data is needed later"],0,"Media with sensitive data must be securely sanitized or physically destroyed, with the disposal documented and the asset register updated. Deleting and reformatting can leave recoverable data.","Domain 4.0: Physical and environmental considerations",[null,"Selling drives as they are would expose confidential customer data to whoever buys them.","Deleting files and reformatting can leave recoverable data, so donating the drives is still risky.","Storing drives in an unlocked cupboard leaves sensitive data unprotected and unaccounted for."],1],["pp90",0,4,"A new expense system is technically ready, but many employees keep using the old spreadsheets and complain that the new tool is confusing. What should the project address?",["Stronger firewall rules around the old spreadsheets","Organizational change management and user training","A larger server to make the system run faster","A change freeze on the expense system for six months"],1,"Adoption problems are people problems: communication about why the change matters, training, and support help users move to the new system. Technical fixes do not solve resistance to change.","Domain 4.0: Governance and change management",["Firewall rules do not address why employees prefer the old spreadsheets over the new tool.",null,"The system is described as technically ready; a bigger server does not fix confusion and resistance.","A change freeze blocks changes and does nothing to help people learn and adopt the new system."],2],["x1",0,1,"While building a new intranet page, a developer adds animated menus and an extra search filter that nobody requested, because he thinks users will like them. What is this called?",["Scope creep","A requirements change","Gold plating","A fast-track"],2,"Gold plating is when the team adds features beyond the agreed scope on its own initiative, without any request. Scope creep comes from uncontrolled requests by stakeholders or users rather than from the team's own additions.","Domain 1: Types of change",["Scope creep comes from uncontrolled requests by others; here the developer added the extras on his own.","A requirements change is a formal update to what is needed; nobody requested these extras.",null,"Fast-tracking is a schedule compression technique of overlapping tasks, not adding features."],1],["x2",0,1,"A security flaw found in production must be patched tonight, before the change control board can meet. How should the project manager handle the change?",["Follow the emergency change process, then document it and have it reviewed after the fact","Apply the patch quietly tonight and leave it out of the change log so the board is not bothered","Wait for the next scheduled board meeting in two weeks","Reject the change because every change needs prior board approval"],0,"Emergency change procedures allow urgent fixes with expedited approval, followed by documentation and a later review. Waiting two weeks would leave a known security flaw open in production.","Domain 1: Change control",[null,"Undocumented changes break the audit trail and can cause confusion or new problems later.","Waiting two weeks leaves a known security flaw exposed in production for no good reason.","Emergency change processes exist precisely so urgent fixes are not blocked by the normal meeting schedule."],2],["x3",0,1,"A project manager identifies a risk that a new data privacy law could affect the entire company's product line, which is far beyond the project's authority. Which risk response is MOST appropriate?",["Accept","Mitigate","Transfer","Escalate"],3,"Escalation is used when a risk is outside the project's scope or the project manager's authority, so it is handed to the right level, such as the program or portfolio owner. Mitigation would mean the project acting on something it has no authority over.","Domain 1: Risk responses",["Accepting would mean the project quietly tolerates a company-wide threat it cannot own.","Mitigation is done by the project within its authority; this risk is beyond it.","Transfer shifts impact to a third party such as an insurer; it does not fit a company-wide regulatory risk.",null],2],["x4",0,1,"A vendor offers a 15% discount if the project orders all its servers before the end of the quarter. The project manager rearranges the schedule to make sure the order is placed in time. Which response to this positive risk is this?",["Enhance","Exploit","Share","Accept"],1,"Exploiting a positive risk means acting to make sure the opportunity definitely happens, such as placing the order in time. Enhancing only raises the probability or impact without guaranteeing it.","Domain 1: Responses to opportunities",["Enhancing increases the chance or size of an opportunity but does not make it certain as this action does.",null,"Sharing means partnering with a third party to capture an opportunity, which is not happening here.","Accepting means taking no special action, while the project manager actively changed the schedule."],2],["x5",0,1,"The team uses money set aside for a known risk that has now occurred: a delayed shipment requiring express freight. Which reserve should normally fund this?",["Management reserve, controlled by senior management","The sponsor's operating budget for next year","Contingency reserve, set aside for identified risks","The vendor's warranty fund"],2,"Contingency reserves are part of the cost baseline and cover identified risks (known unknowns) such as this shipment delay. Management reserves are held outside the baseline for unforeseen work (unknown unknowns) and usually need management approval.","Domain 1: Contingency and management reserves",["Management reserve covers unforeseen risks outside the baseline; this risk was already identified.","Next year's operating budget is not a project reserve and should not fund current project risks.",null,"A warranty covers defective products, not the cost of faster shipping after a delay."],3],["x6",0,1,"For each risk on the register, the project manager names one person who will watch for warning signs and carry out the agreed response. What is this person called?",["Risk owner","Project sponsor","Change control board member","Scrum master"],0,"A risk owner is responsible for monitoring an assigned risk and carrying out its response if it occurs. The sponsor funds and champions the project but does not own each individual risk.","Domain 1: Risk management",[null,"The sponsor provides funding and high-level support, not day-to-day monitoring of individual risks.","Board members approve or reject changes; they are not assigned to watch particular risks.","A scrum master removes impediments for an agile team; owning risks on the register is a separate role."],1],["x7",0,1,"A few months after forming, a project team has agreed on how it works together, and members now support each other's decisions, though they still need some guidance. Which stage of team development is this?",["Forming","Storming","Adjourning","Norming"],3,"In the norming stage, the team settles on shared ways of working and builds trust after the conflict of storming. Performing comes later, when the team works with high independence and needs little direction.","Domain 1: Team development",["Forming is the early polite stage when members are still learning their roles.","Storming involves conflict over roles and approaches, which this team has moved past.","Adjourning happens when the work ends and the team is released.",null],2],["x8",0,1,"Two senior engineers disagree about which database platform to use, and the choice will affect the project for years. The project manager has time available. Which conflict resolution approach is BEST?",["Smoothing, by stressing what they agree on and moving on","Collaborating to find a solution that addresses both engineers' concerns","Forcing, by choosing one option immediately using the project manager's formal authority","Withdrawing, by postponing the decision until the next phase"],1,"Collaborating, or problem solving, works through the underlying concerns to reach a lasting agreement and fits important decisions when there is time. Forcing is quick but can damage commitment and may ignore valid technical points.","Domain 1: Conflict resolution",["Smoothing plays down differences and leaves the real disagreement unresolved on a long-term decision.",null,"Forcing may be quick, but on a long-lasting decision it risks poor buy-in and ignoring valid concerns.","Withdrawing only delays a decision that the project needs, and the conflict will return."],3],["x9",0,1,"A project manager must tell a key stakeholder that her requested feature has been cut from the release. Which communication method is MOST appropriate?",["A live conversation, in person or by video call, followed by a written summary","A line in the weekly status report sent to the whole distribution list, so everyone hears at once","A comment on the project board's task card","A message posted to the team chat channel"],0,"Sensitive or disappointing news should be delivered synchronously and personally so the stakeholder can react and ask questions, then confirmed in writing. Burying it in a mass status report is likely to damage the relationship.","Domain 1: Communication methods",[null,"Learning of a cut feature from a mass report feels dismissive and gives no chance to discuss it.","A task card comment is easy to miss and is too impersonal for disappointing news.","A team channel is public and asynchronous, which is not suitable for sensitive news to one person."],1],["x10",0,1,"At the start of a project, the project manager creates a document listing every stakeholder with their role, contact details, interests and level of influence. What is this document?",["Communication plan","RACI chart","Stakeholder register","Risk register"],2,"The stakeholder register records who the stakeholders are, along with their interests, influence and contact details. The communication plan uses that information to decide what each group receives and when.","Domain 1: Stakeholder management",["A communication plan defines what information goes to whom and how often; it builds on the register.","A RACI chart assigns responsibility for tasks, not stakeholder interests and influence.",null,"A risk register tracks uncertain events and responses, not people and their interests."],1],["x11",0,1,"An organization creates a group that sets project management standards, provides templates and tracks the status of all projects across departments. What is this group?",["Change control board","Project management office (PMO)","Steering committee for one project","Service desk"],1,"A PMO standardizes project governance, supplies templates and methods, and often oversees the portfolio of projects. A change control board reviews change requests rather than setting standards for every project.","Domain 1: Project roles",["A change control board decides on change requests; it does not set standards or track all projects.",null,"A steering committee guides one project or program, not standards for the whole organization.","A service desk handles user support requests, not project governance."],2],["x12",0,1,"Which of these is an operation rather than a project?",["Upgrading all office laptops to a new operating system version by the end of June","Building a new customer portal for a product launch","Moving the data center to a new building next spring","Running the service desk that answers employee IT questions every day"],3,"Operations are ongoing, repetitive work that keeps the business running, such as a daily service desk. Projects are temporary efforts with a defined start and end that create a unique result, like a laptop upgrade by June.","Domain 1: Projects vs operations",["A laptop upgrade with a deadline is temporary and produces a unique result, so it is a project.","Building a new portal has a defined end and a unique outcome, which makes it a project.","A data center move is a one-time effort with a clear finish, so it is a project.",null],1],["x13",0,1,"The sponsor adds a major new requirement but insists that the budget and the deadline cannot change. According to the triple constraint, what is MOST likely to be affected?",["Nothing, because the three constraints are independent","The number of stakeholders on the project","Quality, or other scope must be reduced to make room","Only the communication plan"],2,"Scope, time and cost are linked, so adding scope while fixing time and cost puts pressure on quality or forces other scope to be dropped. The constraints are not independent.","Domain 1: Triple constraint",["The constraints are linked; changing one puts pressure on the others.","Stakeholder numbers are not one of the constraints and would not absorb extra scope.",null,"The communication plan may be updated, but it does not absorb the extra work."],2],["x14",0,1,"In a matrix organization, a network engineer assigned half-time to a project is pulled back to urgent operational work by her functional manager, delaying a project task. What should the project manager do FIRST?",["Discuss the conflict with the functional manager and agree on the engineer's availability","Assign the engineer's work to herself without telling anyone","Remove the engineer from the project permanently and request a full-time replacement from HR","Report the functional manager to the CAB"],0,"In a matrix organization resources are shared, so the project manager should negotiate with the functional manager, escalating to the sponsor only if they cannot agree. The change advisory board approves IT changes and does not resolve staffing conflicts.","Domain 1: Resource management",[null,"Quietly taking on the work hides the problem and is not sustainable.","Removing the engineer loses needed skills and does not address the shared-resource conflict.","The CAB approves production changes; it has no role in resource allocation disputes."],3],["x15",0,1,"Testing can start two days after development starts, and the two then run in parallel. Which relationship and schedule adjustment does this describe?",["Finish-to-start with a two-day lead","Finish-to-finish with a two-day lag","Start-to-finish with no lag","Start-to-start with a two-day lag"],3,"When a successor can begin a set time after its predecessor begins, the relationship is start-to-start with a lag, here two days. A finish-to-start relationship would require development to finish before testing begins.","Domain 1: Dependencies, leads and lags",["Finish-to-start would make testing wait for development to finish, but here they overlap from day two.","Finish-to-finish links the end dates of the two tasks, not their start dates.","Start-to-finish is rare and means the successor cannot finish until the predecessor starts, which is not this case.",null],3],["x16",0,1,"A server installation cannot begin until the building's electrical contractor, who is outside the project, finishes new power circuits. What type of dependency is this?",["Discretionary dependency","External dependency","Internal dependency","Resource leveling dependency"],1,"An external dependency relies on work or parties outside the project, such as a building contractor. A discretionary dependency is a preferred sequence chosen by the team, not something imposed from outside.","Domain 1: Dependencies",["A discretionary dependency is a team's preferred order of work, not a requirement imposed by an outside party.",null,"Internal dependencies are between tasks the project team controls; the contractor is outside the project.","Resource leveling is a scheduling technique, not a type of dependency."],2],["x17",0,1,"With a fixed release date approaching, a product owner labels backlog items as Must have, Should have, Could have and Won't have this time. Which prioritization technique is this?",["MoSCoW","Pareto analysis","Monte Carlo simulation","Critical path method"],0,"MoSCoW sorts requirements into Must, Should, Could and Won't have, helping teams decide what fits a fixed deadline. Pareto analysis finds the few causes behind most problems and is not used to rank requirements this way.","Domain 1: Prioritization",[null,"Pareto analysis ranks causes by frequency to find the vital few, not requirement priority categories.","Monte Carlo simulation models schedule or cost uncertainty, not requirement priority.","The critical path method finds the longest chain of dependent tasks, not which features to include."],2],["x18",0,1,"A Scrum team keeps arguing at sprint reviews about whether stories are really finished. What would MOST help?",["Extending each sprint by one week","Assigning all testing to the next sprint","Letting the product owner decide completion case by case","Agreeing on a shared definition of done for all stories"],3,"A definition of done sets clear, shared criteria that every item must meet to be considered complete, which removes the ambiguity. Case-by-case decisions keep the confusion going and make quality inconsistent.","Domain 1: Agile practices",["Longer sprints do not settle what 'finished' means; the debate would continue.","Pushing testing to later sprints makes stories less finished, not more clearly done.","Case-by-case decisions keep the inconsistency that is causing the arguments.",null],2],["x19",0,1,"At the start of each sprint, the Scrum team selects backlog items it can complete and plans how to deliver them. Which event is this?",["Daily standup","Sprint planning","Sprint retrospective","Backlog refinement"],1,"Sprint planning is where the team decides what it will deliver in the sprint and how. Backlog refinement prepares and estimates items ahead of time but does not commit the sprint's work.","Domain 1: Scrum events",["The daily standup is a short sync on progress and blockers, not planning the whole sprint.",null,"The retrospective happens at the end of a sprint to improve how the team works.","Refinement clarifies and estimates future items; the sprint commitment is made in sprint planning."],1],["x20",0,1,"A company must deliver a data center build with fixed contracts and inspections, but the custom monitoring software for it has unclear requirements. Which approach is MOST suitable?",["Pure waterfall for everything, with all software requirements gathered and frozen now","Pure Scrum for everything, including the construction and inspections","A hybrid approach: predictive for the facility and iterative for the software","No formal methodology, so each team can decide as it goes"],2,"A hybrid approach uses predictive planning where scope is fixed and regulated, and iterative delivery where requirements are uncertain. Freezing unclear software requirements now would likely lead to costly rework.","Domain 1: Choosing a methodology",["Freezing unclear software requirements up front usually leads to rework when needs become clearer.","Construction with fixed contracts and inspections fits poorly with open-ended iterative sprints.",null,"Having no methodology invites confusion and weak control on a complex, contract-bound project."],3],["x21",0,1,"A project team has 6 members and the project manager. The team adds 3 more people. How many total communication channels does the project now have?",["45","21","36","10"],0,"The formula is n(n-1)/2. With 7 people there were 21 channels; with 10 people there are 10 x 9 / 2 = 45. The value 21 is the old total before the new people joined.","Domain 1: Communication channels",[null,"21 is the number of channels for the original 7 people, before the 3 new members joined.","36 would be the channels for 9 people; the new total is 10 people including the project manager.","10 is the number of people, not the number of communication channels between them."],3],["x22",0,2,"A company hires a vendor to install a wireless network. Which document describes in detail the work the vendor will perform, the deliverables and the acceptance criteria?",["Project charter","Business case","Memorandum of understanding (MOU)","Statement of work (SOW)"],3,"A statement of work details the work, deliverables, timeline and acceptance criteria for a vendor. An MOU records a general, usually non-binding intent to cooperate rather than detailed work.","Domain 2: Procurement documents",["The charter authorizes the project internally; it does not define a vendor's detailed work.","A business case justifies the investment; it does not describe vendor tasks and deliverables.","An MOU states a general intent to cooperate and usually lacks detailed, enforceable work terms.",null],1],["x23",0,2,"Two internal departments agree to share a server room for a project. They want to write down their shared intentions and responsibilities, but not create a legally binding contract. Which document fits BEST?",["Master service agreement","Memorandum of understanding (MOU)","Purchase order","Non-disclosure agreement"],1,"An MOU records shared intentions and responsibilities between parties without the force of a binding contract, which suits two internal departments. A master service agreement is a binding contract that sets terms for future vendor work.","Domain 2: Agreements",["A master service agreement is a binding contract for ongoing vendor work, more formal than needed here.",null,"A purchase order authorizes buying goods or services from a vendor, not sharing space internally.","An NDA protects confidential information; it does not describe shared responsibilities for a room."],2],["x24",0,2,"Midway through testing, an auditor asks the team to prove that every approved requirement has a matching design element and test case. Which document helps MOST?",["Project communication management plan","Resource calendar","Requirements traceability matrix","Issue log"],2,"A requirements traceability matrix links each requirement to its source, design, build and test items, showing that nothing was missed. An issue log tracks current problems, not coverage of requirements.","Domain 2: Requirements management",["The communication plan covers who receives which information, not requirement coverage.","A resource calendar shows when people and equipment are available, not requirement links.",null,"An issue log tracks problems and their owners; it does not map requirements to tests."],2],["x25",0,2,"Stakeholders keep assuming a website redesign will also migrate the old blog archive, which was never planned. What should the project scope statement include to prevent this?",["Explicit exclusions listing what is out of scope","A longer list of team members","The detailed test scripts","The vendor's invoice schedule and payment milestones for the redesign"],0,"A scope statement should list both what is included and what is explicitly excluded, which prevents wrong assumptions such as expecting the blog archive migration. Test scripts and invoices do not clarify scope boundaries.","Domain 2: Scope definition",[null,"Listing more team members says nothing about what work is included or excluded.","Test scripts verify work, but they do not set the boundaries of the project's scope.","An invoice schedule covers payments, not which deliverables are in or out of scope."],2],["x26",0,2,"A project manager marks 'Network cutover complete' on the schedule as a point in time with zero duration that shows an important event has been reached. What is this?",["A work package","A dependency","A sprint","A milestone"],3,"A milestone is a significant point or event in the schedule with no duration. A work package is a unit of work at the lowest level of the WBS that takes time and effort.","Domain 2: Scheduling",["A work package is actual work with effort and duration, not a zero-duration marker.","A dependency is a relationship between tasks, not a point in time.","A sprint is a time-boxed iteration of work lasting weeks, not a single point in time.",null],1],["x27",0,2,"At the end of the design phase, the sponsor and steering committee review deliverables and decide whether the project may proceed to the build phase. What is this review called?",["Daily standup","Phase gate review","Sprint retrospective","Lessons learned session"],1,"A phase gate (or stage gate) review is a formal checkpoint where leaders decide whether to continue, change or stop the project before the next phase. A lessons learned session captures experience but does not authorize moving forward.","Domain 2: Project governance",["A daily standup is a short team sync, not a formal go or no-go decision by leadership.",null,"A retrospective improves the team's process; it does not decide whether the project proceeds.","Lessons learned capture experience for the future and do not approve the next phase."],2],["x28",0,2,"A hospital is replacing its patient scheduling system, and a failure would disrupt care. It wants to run the old and new systems side by side for a month and compare results before switching off the old one. Which deployment approach is this?",["Big bang cutover","Phased rollout by department","Parallel run","Pilot with one volunteer clinic"],2,"A parallel run keeps both systems working at the same time so results can be compared and the old system can take over if problems appear. A big bang switches everyone at once, which carries the highest risk.","Domain 2: Deployment approaches",["A big bang switches everyone on one date with no fallback running, the opposite of this plan.","A phased rollout moves groups one after another; it does not run both systems for everyone.",null,"A pilot tests with a small group first, but here both systems run together across the hospital."],2],["x29",0,2,"Before rolling a new VPN client out to 3,000 employees, the team deploys it to one small office for two weeks to find problems early. What is this approach called?",["Pilot","Parallel run","Big bang","Crashing"],0,"A pilot releases a solution to a small, representative group first so issues can be fixed before wider rollout. A parallel run keeps the old and new systems running together rather than testing on a subset of users.","Domain 2: Deployment approaches",[null,"A parallel run operates both old and new systems at once; it is not limited to a small group.","Big bang deploys to everyone at once, which is the opposite of starting small.","Crashing adds resources to shorten the schedule; it is not a deployment approach."],2],["x30",0,2,"Three vendors bid for a help desk contract. The team scores each on cost, experience and support hours, using weights agreed in advance, and picks the highest total. What is this tool?",["Pareto chart","Risk register","Burndown chart","Weighted scoring model"],3,"A weighted scoring model rates options against weighted criteria so vendor selection is objective and defensible. Choosing only on lowest cost would ignore the experience and support factors the team cares about.","Domain 2: Vendor selection",["A Pareto chart ranks causes by frequency; it does not score vendors against criteria.","A risk register tracks uncertain events, not vendor evaluations.","A burndown chart tracks remaining work over time, not vendor comparisons.",null],3],["x31",0,2,"After a vendor is chosen, the organization issues a formal document authorizing the purchase of 50 switches at an agreed price. What is this document?",["Request for information (RFI)","Purchase order (PO)","Request for proposal (RFP)","Statement of work (SOW)"],1,"A purchase order is the buyer's official authorization to buy specific goods or services at an agreed price. An RFI or RFP is used earlier, to gather information or proposals before a vendor is chosen.","Domain 2: Procurement",["An RFI gathers general information from vendors early on and does not authorize a purchase.",null,"An RFP asks vendors for proposals before selection; it does not place an order.","A SOW describes work to be performed, mainly for services, and is not the purchase authorization itself."],1],["x32",0,2,"A project is ending, and the senior database specialist who configured the new system is moving to another project next week. What should the project manager arrange before she leaves?",["Nothing, because the documentation will be written after she leaves","A change freeze on all company systems until the specialist has fully left the project","Knowledge transfer to the operations team, with documented procedures","A new request for proposal"],2,"Knowledge transfer ensures the operations team can support the system after key experts leave, usually through documentation, walkthroughs and shadowing. Waiting until she has gone risks losing knowledge that only she holds.","Domain 2: Closing and transition",["Once the specialist leaves, the knowledge she holds may be lost or hard to obtain.","A company-wide change freeze does not capture her knowledge and would disrupt other work.",null,"An RFP is for buying from vendors; it does not preserve the specialist's knowledge."],2],["x33",0,2,"All deliverables are accepted and lessons learned are recorded. Which activity formally completes project closure?",["Obtaining formal sign-off and releasing the project's resources","Starting a new sprint to add the optional features the customer mentioned during acceptance","Rebaselining the schedule","Opening a new change request for the final report"],0,"Closing ends with formal sign-off from the sponsor or customer and releasing people and other resources. Starting new work after acceptance would reopen the project rather than close it.","Domain 2: Project closure",[null,"Adding features after acceptance is new work and would reopen scope instead of closing.","Rebaselining is done during execution when approved changes occur, not to finish a project.","A final report does not need a change request; it is a normal closing document."],1],["x34",0,2,"Two projects compete for funding. Project A has a net present value (NPV) of $80,000 over 5 years. Project B has an NPV of $50,000 over 3 years. Based only on NPV, which should be selected?",["Project B, because its duration is shorter","Either one, because NPV cannot compare projects of different lengths","Project B, because NPV should be divided by years before comparing","Project A, because it has the higher NPV"],3,"NPV already converts future cash flows to today's value, so the project with the higher NPV is preferred; duration is already accounted for. Dividing NPV by the number of years is not a standard selection rule.","Domain 2: Project selection",["NPV already accounts for the timing of cash flows, so a shorter duration is not a reason to choose B.","NPV is expressed in today's money, which is what makes projects of different lengths comparable.","Dividing NPV by years is not a standard method and would distort the comparison.",null],3],["x35",0,2,"During execution, a key stakeholder asks why a design decision made three months ago was chosen over an alternative. Which document should the project manager consult?",["The resource calendar","The decision log","The procurement statement of work","The risk probability and impact matrix"],1,"A decision log records key decisions with the date, who decided, the options considered and the reasons. The risk matrix rates risks and does not explain why a design option was chosen.","Domain 2: Project documentation",["A resource calendar shows availability of people and equipment, not the reasons behind decisions.",null,"The SOW describes vendor work; it does not record internal design decisions and their reasons.","A probability and impact matrix rates risks and does not document design choices."],3],["x36",0,2,"The project charter has just been approved. Which activity should the project manager perform NEXT to understand who can affect or be affected by the project?",["Close the vendor contracts","Hold the lessons learned session","Identify and analyze stakeholders","Perform user acceptance testing"],2,"Right after initiation, identifying and analyzing stakeholders lets the project manager plan engagement and communication. User acceptance testing and contract closure happen much later in the life cycle.","Domain 2: Initiating",["Contracts are closed at the end of the project, not right after the charter is approved.","Lessons learned are gathered throughout and at closing, not as the next step after approval.",null,"UAT happens after deliverables are built, much later than initiation."],2],["x37",0,2,"A project's final cost estimate is $400,000. The sponsor approves a cost baseline of $400,000 plus a $40,000 contingency reserve for identified risks, and holds a separate $20,000 management reserve. What is the project budget?",["$420,000","$440,000","$400,000","$460,000"],3,"The cost baseline including contingency is $440,000, and adding the $20,000 management reserve gives a total project budget of $460,000. The $440,000 figure is the cost baseline, which excludes the management reserve.","Domain 2: Cost baseline and budget",["$420,000 adds only the management reserve and leaves out the contingency reserve.","$440,000 is the cost baseline with contingency; the total budget also includes the management reserve.","$400,000 leaves out both the contingency and management reserves.",null],3],["x38",0,2,"During planning, the project manager assembles one document that explains how the project will be executed, monitored, controlled and closed, bringing together the scope, schedule, cost, risk and communication plans. What is it?",["Project charter","Project management plan","Business case","Lessons learned register"],1,"The project management plan integrates the subsidiary plans and baselines and describes how the project will be run and controlled. The charter comes earlier and only authorizes the project at a high level.","Domain 2: Planning",["The charter authorizes the project at a high level; it does not integrate the detailed subsidiary plans.",null,"The business case justifies the investment; it does not describe how the project will be managed.","The lessons learned register records experience, not the plans for running the project."],2],["x39",0,3,"An agile team wants a visual board with columns such as To Do, In Progress and Done, where cards move across as work advances. What tool is this?",["Gantt chart","Kanban board","Fishbone diagram","Histogram"],1,"A Kanban board shows work items as cards moving through workflow columns, making status and bottlenecks visible. A Gantt chart shows tasks as bars against a calendar rather than cards moving through stages.","Domain 3: Visual tools",["A Gantt chart plots tasks on a timeline; it does not move cards between workflow columns.",null,"A fishbone diagram groups possible causes of a problem, not the status of work items.","A histogram shows frequency distributions, not the flow of work."],1],["x40",0,3,"A project manager wants to see how many hours each team member is scheduled to work per week, to spot anyone who is overallocated. Which chart fits BEST?",["Scatter diagram","Pareto chart","Burnup chart","Resource histogram"],3,"A resource histogram shows the hours planned for each resource per period, making over-allocation easy to spot. A burnup chart shows completed work against total scope, not individual workloads.","Domain 3: Resource tools",["A scatter diagram shows the relationship between two variables, not weekly workload per person.","A Pareto chart ranks causes by frequency; it does not show people's scheduled hours.","A burnup chart tracks completed work and total scope, not individual allocations.",null],2],["x41",0,3,"A project manager needs one log that tracks Risks, Assumptions, Issues and Dependencies together for weekly review. What is this commonly called?",["RAID log","RACI chart","WBS dictionary","Burndown chart"],0,"A RAID log tracks Risks, Assumptions, Issues and Dependencies in one place. A RACI chart assigns who is Responsible, Accountable, Consulted and Informed, which is a different purpose.","Domain 3: Project logs",[null,"A RACI chart defines roles for tasks; it does not track risks, issues or dependencies.","A WBS dictionary describes work packages, not risks and issues.","A burndown chart shows remaining work over time, not a log of risks and issues."],1],["x42",0,3,"A project has a budget at completion (BAC) of $300,000. The current estimate at completion (EAC) is $360,000, and actual cost (AC) to date is $150,000. What is the estimate to complete (ETC)?",["$60,000","$150,000","$210,000","$510,000"],2,"ETC = EAC - AC = $360,000 - $150,000 = $210,000, the expected cost of the remaining work. $60,000 is the variance at completion (BAC - EAC), not the cost still to be spent.","Domain 3: Earned value forecasting",["$60,000 is the variance at completion (BAC - EAC), not the remaining cost.","$150,000 is what has already been spent, not what remains to be spent.",null,"$510,000 wrongly adds AC to EAC; EAC already includes the money spent to date."],3],["x43",0,3,"At a status review, a project shows CPI = 1.1 and SPI = 0.85. What is the BEST interpretation?",["Over budget and ahead of schedule","Under budget but behind schedule","Over budget and behind schedule","Under budget and ahead of schedule"],1,"A CPI above 1 means more value is earned per dollar spent (under budget), and an SPI below 1 means less work is done than planned (behind schedule). The project may be spending less partly because work is lagging.","Domain 3: Earned value analysis",["A CPI above 1 means under budget, and an SPI below 1 means behind, so both parts are reversed.",null,"CPI above 1 shows the project is under budget, not over.","SPI below 1 shows the project is behind schedule, not ahead."],3],["x44",0,3,"A project has planned value (PV) of $120,000, earned value (EV) of $100,000 and actual cost (AC) of $95,000. What is the schedule variance (SV)?",["-$20,000","$5,000","-$25,000","$20,000"],0,"SV = EV - PV = $100,000 - $120,000 = -$20,000, so the project is behind schedule. $5,000 is the cost variance (EV - AC), which measures cost, not schedule.","Domain 3: Earned value analysis",[null,"$5,000 is the cost variance (EV - AC), not the schedule variance.","-$25,000 compares AC with PV, which is not a standard earned value formula.","$20,000 has the wrong sign; EV is lower than PV, so the variance is negative."],3],["x45",0,3,"Several team members keep editing old copies of the project plan and requirements, causing confusion about which version is current. What should the project manager put in place?",["A larger email distribution list so every update reaches everyone at once","A daily standup meeting","A new RACI chart","A central document repository with version control"],3,"A shared repository with version control keeps one current copy, tracks changes and shows history. More email only spreads more copies and makes the confusion worse.","Domain 3: Document management",["Sending documents to more people by email creates even more uncontrolled copies.","A standup improves coordination but does not stop people editing outdated files.","A RACI chart clarifies roles but does not control document versions.",null],2],["x46",0,3,"At the start of a brainstorming session, a team wants to capture ideas visually around a central topic, with related ideas branching out from it. Which tool fits BEST?",["Control chart","Earned value report","Mind map","Resource calendar"],2,"A mind map places a central topic in the middle with related ideas branching outward, which suits brainstorming and organizing thoughts. A control chart monitors process stability over time and is not an idea-generation tool.","Domain 3: Brainstorming tools",["A control chart tracks whether a process stays within limits, not ideas during brainstorming.","Earned value reports measure cost and schedule performance, not ideas.",null,"A resource calendar shows availability, not a structure of ideas."],2],["x47",0,3,"Every Friday, the project manager sends stakeholders a short document summarizing progress, completed milestones, upcoming work, and current risks and issues. What is this document?",["Project charter","Status report","Statement of work","Business case"],1,"A status report regularly updates stakeholders on progress, upcoming work, risks and issues. The project charter authorizes the project once at the start and is not a recurring update.","Domain 3: Project reporting",["The charter is created once to authorize the project, not sent every week.",null,"A SOW defines work for a vendor and is not a weekly progress update.","A business case justifies the investment before the project starts, not weekly progress."],1],["x48",0,3,"For a task, the optimistic estimate is 3 days and the pessimistic estimate is 15 days. Using the three-point (PERT) approach, what is the standard deviation?",["2 days","12 days","6 days","4 days"],0,"PERT standard deviation = (pessimistic - optimistic) / 6 = (15 - 3) / 6 = 2 days. The 12-day figure is simply the range between the two estimates, not divided by six.","Domain 3: Estimation",[null,"12 days is the range between pessimistic and optimistic before dividing by 6.","6 days divides the range by 2 instead of by 6.","4 days divides the range by 3, which is not the PERT formula."],3],["x49",0,3,"A service desk team tracks each user request from submission through assignment to resolution, with priorities and response-time targets. Which tool are they using?",["Mind map","Pareto chart","Ticketing system","Gantt chart"],2,"A ticketing system records, assigns, prioritizes and tracks requests through to resolution against targets. A Gantt chart schedules planned project tasks and is not designed to handle a stream of user requests.","Domain 3: Productivity and tracking tools",["A mind map organizes ideas; it does not track individual requests through resolution.","A Pareto chart can analyze ticket data, but it does not record or assign requests.",null,"A Gantt chart plans scheduled tasks, not incoming requests with response targets."],2],["x50",0,4,"A project team handles salary data for all employees. The company labels the data so everyone knows it may be seen only by authorized HR staff. What is this practice called?",["Change control","Resource leveling","Load balancing","Data classification"],3,"Data classification labels information by sensitivity, such as public, internal or confidential, so the right handling and access controls are applied. Change control governs modifications to systems, not how sensitive data is labeled.","Domain 4: Data security",["Change control manages changes to systems and scope, not sensitivity labels on data.","Resource leveling adjusts schedules around resource limits, not data handling.","Load balancing spreads network or server traffic, not labels on sensitive information.",null],1],["x51",0,4,"On a finance system project, the same developer who writes payment code is also able to approve and deploy it to production without review. Which governance principle is missing?",["Separation (segregation) of duties","Fast-tracking","Gold plating","Resource smoothing within available float"],0,"Separation of duties splits critical steps, such as writing, approving and deploying code, among different people to reduce fraud and error. Fast-tracking is a schedule technique and has nothing to do with controls over who approves changes.","Domain 4: Governance controls",[null,"Fast-tracking overlaps tasks to save time; it is not a control over who can approve changes.","Gold plating means adding unrequested features, not a lack of approval controls.","Resource smoothing adjusts workloads within float; it is not a security control."],2],["x52",0,4,"A vendor's technicians will install equipment in the company's server room for two days. What is the BEST physical security practice?",["Give them a permanent badge with access to all areas so they are never delayed","Leave the server room door propped open so they can come and go","Sign them in, issue temporary badges and escort them in the server room","Let them share an employee's badge for the two days"],2,"Visitors should be logged, given time-limited badges and escorted in sensitive areas so their access is controlled and traceable. Sharing an employee's badge breaks accountability because actions cannot be traced to the right person.","Domain 4: Physical security",["Permanent all-area access is far more than two days of work in one room requires.","A propped-open door lets anyone into the server room, not just the vendor.",null,"Badge sharing breaks accountability because entries are recorded under the wrong person."],1],["x53",0,4,"A business states that its order system must be running again within four hours of a major outage. Which metric does this define?",["Recovery point objective (RPO)","Recovery time objective (RTO)","Mean time between failures (MTBF)","Service level indicator (SLI)"],1,"RTO is the maximum acceptable time to restore a system after a disruption, here four hours. RPO is about how much data loss is acceptable, measured back in time from the failure.","Domain 4: Business continuity",["RPO measures acceptable data loss, not how long restoration may take.",null,"MTBF measures average time between failures, not the allowed restoration time.","An SLI is a measured service metric, not a recovery target after an outage."],2],["x54",0,4,"What is the main difference between a business continuity plan (BCP) and a disaster recovery plan (DRP)?",["They are two names for the same document","A DRP covers how the whole organization keeps operating, while a BCP covers only restoring IT systems and backups","A BCP is required only for cloud systems","A BCP keeps critical business functions running, while a DRP focuses on restoring IT systems and data"],3,"The BCP covers how the whole business keeps critical functions going during a disruption, and the DRP is the IT-focused part for restoring systems and data. The option that says the DRP covers the whole organization reverses their scope.","Domain 4: Continuity and recovery",["They are related but different; the DRP is usually a component of the broader continuity effort.","This reverses the two: the BCP is broad and the DRP is IT-focused.","Continuity planning applies to all critical functions, whether on-premises or in the cloud.",null],3],["x55",0,4,"A project's software changes move from developers' machines to a shared environment where testers check integrated features, then to staging, then to production. Why are separate environments used?",["To find and fix problems before changes reach live users","To give every developer direct access to production","To avoid the need for a rollback plan","To make the change advisory board unnecessary"],0,"Separate development, test and staging environments let issues be caught before they affect production users. They reduce risk but do not replace rollback plans or change approval.","Domain 4: Environments",[null,"Separate environments exist partly to limit direct changes to production, not to widen access.","Changes can still fail in production, so a rollback plan is still required.","Production changes still need approval; test environments do not replace governance."],2],["x56",0,4,"A project will buy 200 licenses for a design application. Midway through, the team plans to install it on 260 computers because some staff share machines. What is the MAIN concern?",["The software will run noticeably more slowly on machines that several employees share during the day","Installing beyond the licensed count may breach the license agreement","Shared machines cannot join the domain","The change log will become too long"],1,"Installing software beyond what the license permits can breach the agreement and expose the company to audits and penalties, so licensing terms must be checked first. Performance on shared machines is a minor concern next to compliance.","Domain 4: Software licensing",["Performance may vary, but the key risk here is legal and financial non-compliance.",null,"Domain membership is unrelated to how many licenses were purchased.","Log length is not a real concern compared with breaching the license terms."],3],["x57",0,4,"A critical production server fails on a weekend, and an urgent fix is needed before the next regular change meeting. Who typically approves such a change under ITIL-style change management?",["Any developer on the team, without approval","The vendor's sales representative","The project's end users by vote","An emergency change advisory board (ECAB)"],3,"An emergency CAB is a smaller group that can quickly approve urgent changes outside the normal schedule. Letting any developer deploy without approval bypasses controls and increases the risk of making things worse.","Domain 4: Change management",["Unapproved changes bypass controls and can cause further outages.","A sales representative has no authority over the company's production changes.","End users do not approve production changes; that is a governance function.",null],2],["x58",0,4,"A project will collect customers' names, email addresses and dates of birth for a loyalty program. What should the project manager ensure FIRST regarding this data?",["That the data is stored on the fastest available disks","That every team member can export it to spreadsheets","That privacy requirements are identified and only the data needed is collected, with proper consent and protection","That the data is kept forever in case the marketing team finds a new use for it later, even after customers leave the program"],2,"Personal data must be handled under applicable privacy rules: collect only what is needed, get appropriate consent, protect it and set retention limits. Keeping it forever conflicts with data minimization and retention principles.","Domain 4: Data privacy",["Storage speed is a technical detail; privacy obligations come first with personal data.","Broad export access increases the risk of leaks and violates least privilege.",null,"Keeping personal data forever conflicts with retention limits and data minimization."],3],["x59",0,4,"A project will migrate the company's file shares next month. Backups have run every night for years, but no one remembers restoring from them. What should the project manager insist on before cutover?",["Doubling the backup frequency without changing anything else","Deleting older backups to free up storage for the migration","A test restore to confirm that the backups can actually be recovered","Switching to a different backup vendor before the migration"],2,"A backup is only useful if it can be restored, so a test restore before a risky change confirms that recovery will work. Running backups more often does not prove that any of them can be restored.","Domain 4: Backups and recovery",["More frequent backups do not prove that any of them can actually be restored.","Deleting backups before a risky migration removes recovery options when they may be needed most.",null,"Changing vendors right before cutover adds risk and still does not verify recovery."],2],["x60",0,4,"A company plans to move a workload from its own servers to a public cloud. From a budgeting view, how does the cost model MOST likely change?",["From up-front capital expenses toward ongoing operating expenses based on usage","From operating expenses to large up-front hardware purchases","Costs disappear because the provider handles all of the hardware, staffing and maintenance","Costs become fixed and no longer need monitoring"],0,"Public cloud usually shifts spending from buying hardware (CapEx) to paying for use over time (OpEx), which still needs monitoring because usage can grow. Costs do not disappear; they change form.","Domain 4: Infrastructure cost models",[null,"This reverses the shift; cloud generally reduces up-front hardware purchases.","The provider runs the infrastructure but charges for it, so costs remain.","Cloud costs vary with usage and need ongoing monitoring to avoid overspending."],3]]);
